(window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS = window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS || {})[9] = {"grade":9,"packs":{"grade9-biology":{"g9s-b1-circulatory":[{"id":"g9s-b1-v005","q":"Blood that has been oxygenated in the lungs returns to the heart through which vessel?","opts":["The pulmonary vein","The pulmonary artery","The vena cava","The coronary vein"],"a":"The pulmonary vein","hint":"The name contains the word \"pulmonary\" for lungs, and it is a vein (to the heart).","exp":"The pulmonary vein carries freshly oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium."},{"id":"g9s-b1-v008","q":"Which vessel carries deoxygenated blood from the whole body back to the right atrium?","opts":["The pulmonary vein","The vena cava","The aorta","The coronary vein"],"a":"The vena cava","hint":"There are two of them — superior and inferior.","exp":"The vena cava (superior from the upper body, inferior from the lower body) returns deoxygenated blood to the right atrium."},{"id":"g9s-bpi-017","q":"The heart has four chambers. Which chamber pumps oxygenated blood to the rest of the body via the aorta?","opts":["Right ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium","Left ventricle"],"a":"Left ventricle","hint":"The left side of the heart deals with oxygenated blood; ventricles pump blood OUT of the heart.","exp":"The left ventricle receives oxygenated blood from the left atrium and pumps it under high pressure into the aorta for distribution to all body tissues."},{"id":"g9s-bhard-005","q":"Haemoglobin picks up oxygen in the lungs to form oxyhaemoglobin. In the body tissues, oxyhaemoglobin releases the oxygen. Which conditions in body tissues trigger this release?","opts":["High CO₂ concentration and low oxygen concentration","Low CO₂ concentration and high oxygen concentration","Low temperature and high atmospheric pressure","High pH and low blood-flow rate"],"a":"High CO₂ concentration and low oxygen concentration","hint":"Active tissues produce CO₂ and use up O₂. How does this affect haemoglobin?","exp":"In respiring tissues, oxygen is consumed (lowering O₂ concentration) and CO₂ is produced. The high CO₂ lowers pH (the Bohr effect), which reduces haemoglobin's affinity for oxygen. As a result, <b>oxyhaemoglobin releases O₂</b> to the cells that need it. The reverse happens in the lungs: low CO₂ and high O₂ cause haemoglobin to load up with oxygen."},{"id":"g9s-b1-029","q":"The graph shows deaths rising with salt intake. What does that NOT prove on its own?","opts":["That the pattern is the same across the range","That the salt is what causes the deaths","That the two measurements rise together","That deaths are higher at 10 g than at 2 g"],"a":"That the salt is what causes the deaths","hint":"Two things rising together is not the same as one causing the other.","exp":"The graph shows a correlation; proving cause needs further evidence, because something else could explain both."}],"g9s-b2-reproductive":[{"id":"g9s-b2-002","q":"Why is reproduction important for a species?","opts":["Without it the species would die out","It provides the energy each individual needs","It removes waste products from the body","It allows each individual to live longer"],"a":"Without it the species would die out","hint":"Think about what happens over generations.","exp":"Individuals die, so a species survives only if new individuals are produced to replace them."},{"id":"g9s-b2-007","q":"What is the main advantage of asexual reproduction?","opts":["It needs two parents to find each other","Only one parent is needed, so it can be very fast","It always produces stronger offspring","The offspring are all genetically different"],"a":"Only one parent is needed, so it can be very fast","hint":"No partner has to be found.","exp":"With one parent and no need to find a mate, asexual reproduction can produce many offspring quickly."},{"id":"g9s-btask-008-a","q":"Name the organ where an egg cell is produced and released in the female reproductive system.","opts":["The uterus","The oviduct (fallopian tube)","The ovary","The cervix"],"a":"The ovary","hint":"There are two of them, one on each side.","exp":"The ovary produces and releases egg cells (ova). There are two ovaries, one on each side of the uterus."},{"id":"g9s-b2-016","q":"What is <b>fertilisation</b>?","opts":["The release of an egg cell from the ovary","The joining of a sperm cell and an egg cell","The production of the sperm inside the testes","The growth of the baby inside the uterus"],"a":"The joining of a sperm cell and an egg cell","hint":"Two cells become one.","exp":"Fertilisation is the fusion of the male and female sex cells to form a single new cell."},{"id":"g9s-b2-021","q":"Which measure best prevents the spread of sexually transmitted diseases?","opts":["Boiling all drinking water before use","Avoiding shaking hands with other people","Avoiding unprotected sexual contact","Sleeping under a mosquito net at night"],"a":"Avoiding unprotected sexual contact","hint":"Match the prevention to the way the disease is passed on.","exp":"STDs are passed on through sexual contact, so avoiding unprotected contact is the measure that works."}],"g9s-b3-biodiversity":[{"id":"g9s-b3-v003","q":"A scientist notes that the individuals in a population of Mauritian pink pigeons have slightly different beak sizes and feather patterns. Which type of biodiversity does this illustrate?","opts":["Species diversity across a habitat","Habitat diversity across the island","Ecosystem diversity across Mauritius","Genetic diversity within a species"],"a":"Genetic diversity within a species","hint":"The differences are between individuals of the same species.","exp":"Genetic diversity refers to the variation in genes between individuals of the same species; different beak sizes and feather patterns in a pigeon population arise from genetic differences."},{"id":"g9s-bmy-022","q":"Which of the following can be estimated using a <b>quadrat</b>?","opts":["Birds","Fish","Dogs","Algae"],"a":"Algae","hint":"","exp":"Quadrats are placed randomly in an area to count organisms that do not move, such as plants and algae. Mobile animals like birds, dogs and fish cannot be sampled using a stationary quadrat."},{"id":"g9s-pp24b-014","q":"Which pair are <b>natural calamities</b> that affect biodiversity?","opts":["Cyclone and pollution","Deforestation and drought","Cyclone and drought","Pollution and deforestation"],"a":"Cyclone and drought","hint":"Which two happen without any human action?","exp":"A <b>cyclone</b> and a <b>drought</b> are natural events. Pollution and deforestation are caused by people, so they are human activities rather than natural calamities."},{"id":"g9s-bhard-007","q":"Which of the following human activities directly causes habitat loss and is widely regarded as the single greatest threat to global biodiversity?","opts":["Deforestation and land clearance for agriculture","Release of greenhouse gases into the atmosphere","Hunting and poaching of large animals","Overuse of chemical pesticides on farms"],"a":"Deforestation and land clearance for agriculture","hint":"Which activity physically destroys the places where species live?","exp":"<b>Deforestation and land clearance</b> directly destroy habitats — the places where species feed, breed and shelter. Without habitat, populations cannot survive. Agriculture (including cattle ranching and palm-oil cultivation) is responsible for over 80 % of tropical deforestation globally. Pesticides, hunting and climate change also threaten biodiversity but affect it more indirectly or selectively."},{"id":"g9s-b3-011","q":"Why is the count from several quadrats averaged before the estimate is made?","opts":["Averaging makes the number smaller and easier to use","One quadrat may not be typical of the whole area","The quadrat changes size each time it is used","It is the only way to make the count a whole number"],"a":"One quadrat may not be typical of the whole area","hint":"Think about how much one square metre can tell you.","exp":"Several samples averaged give a far more reliable estimate than any single square."}]},"grade9-chemistry":{"g9s-c1-atmosphere":[{"id":"g9s-c1-012","q":"Which pollutant is released mainly by burning coal that contains sulfur?","opts":["Carbon monoxide","Methane","CFCs","Sulfur dioxide"],"a":"Sulfur dioxide","hint":"The sulfur in the fuel combines with oxygen.","exp":"Sulfur in the coal burns to sulfur dioxide, one of the two main causes of acid rain."},{"id":"g9s-cpi-017","q":"A catalytic converter also converts nitrogen monoxide (NO) from car exhausts into which harmless gas?","opts":["Ammonia (NH₃)","Nitrogen gas (N₂)","Nitrogen dioxide (NO₂)","Dinitrogen oxide (N₂O)"],"a":"Nitrogen gas (N₂)","hint":"","exp":"2NO → N₂ + O₂. The catalytic converter reduces NO back to harmless nitrogen gas, which makes up 78% of the atmosphere."},{"id":"g9s-cpi-010","q":"Which of the following is NOT a consequence of global warming?","opts":["Loss of natural habitats for wildlife","More frequent and intense extreme weather events","Coral bleaching in warm ocean water","Thickening of the polar ice caps"],"a":"Thickening of the polar ice caps","hint":"","exp":"Global warming causes polar ice caps to melt and thin — not thicken. Coral bleaching, habitat loss and increased extreme weather events are all real consequences."},{"id":"g9s-c1-024","q":"Which measure would most directly reduce acid rain?","opts":["Adding lime to the chimneys of every house","Building the chimneys of power stations taller","Burning the same fuel more quickly","Removing sulfur from fuels before they are burned"],"a":"Removing sulfur from fuels before they are burned","hint":"Deal with the cause, not with where it lands.","exp":"Taking the sulfur out before burning stops the sulfur dioxide being made at all; a taller chimney only moves it."},{"id":"g9s-c1-019","q":"Which human activity has added most carbon dioxide to the atmosphere?","opts":["Fishing in the open sea","Planting forests","Building roads out of stone","Burning fossil fuels"],"a":"Burning fossil fuels","hint":"Which of these releases carbon that was locked away?","exp":"Burning coal, oil and gas releases carbon that had been stored underground for millions of years."}],"g9s-c2-mixtures":[{"id":"g9s-c2-021","q":"Which technique obtains pure water from salt water?","opts":["Magnetism","Sublimation","Filtration","Distillation"],"a":"Distillation","hint":"The salt is dissolved, so a paper will not stop it.","exp":"Distillation boils the water off and condenses it, leaving the dissolved salt behind."},{"id":"g9s-cdm-002","q":"A diagram of a <b>simple distillation</b> apparatus shows a round-bottom flask labelled \"Mixture + boiling chips\" being heated by a Bunsen burner. Why are <b>boiling chips</b> added to the mixture?","opts":["To decrease the boiling point of the mixture","To increase the boiling point of the mixture","To ensure smooth boiling of the mixture","To prevent the mixture from cooling down"],"a":"To ensure smooth boiling of the mixture","hint":"Without them, the liquid may \"bump\" violently instead of boiling smoothly.","exp":"<b>Boiling chips (anti-bumping granules)</b> are added to ensure <b>smooth, even boiling</b>. They provide nucleation sites for bubbles to form gradually. Without them, the liquid can become superheated and suddenly boil violently (\"bumping\"), which could break the glassware or cause spillage."},{"id":"g9s-c2-020","q":"Which technique separates sand from water?","opts":["Sublimation","Distillation","Filtration","Crystallisation"],"a":"Filtration","hint":"Sand does not dissolve.","exp":"Sand is insoluble, so it is trapped by a filter paper while the water passes through."},{"id":"g9s-pp24c-012","q":"Which separation technique is used to obtain <b>iodine from a mixture of iodine and sand</b>?","opts":["Filtration","Crystallisation","Distillation","Sublimation"],"a":"Sublimation","hint":"Iodine turns straight from solid to vapour when heated, without melting.","exp":"Iodine <b>sublimes</b> - it goes from solid to vapour and back to solid without becoming a liquid - while sand does not. Heating the mixture and collecting the deposit separates them."},{"id":"g9s-c2-018","q":"What is filtration used to separate?","opts":["A dissolved solid from its solution","Two gases from the air","An insoluble solid from a liquid","Two liquids with different boiling points"],"a":"An insoluble solid from a liquid","hint":"Would a dissolved solid be stopped by the paper?","exp":"Filtration removes an insoluble solid; anything dissolved passes straight through the paper with the liquid."}],"g9s-c3-language":[{"id":"g9s-c3-008","q":"How many oxygen atoms are there in one formula unit of Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>?","opts":["4","2","3","1"],"a":"2","hint":"The bracket subscript multiplies everything inside it.","exp":"The 2 outside the bracket applies to both the O and the H, so there are 2 oxygen atoms."},{"id":"g9s-cfam-006-c","q":"A metal M forms a sulfate with the formula M₂SO₄. Sulfate ions are SO₄²⁻. What is the formula of the chloride of M?","opts":["M₂Cl","MCl₂","MCl","MCl₃"],"a":"MCl","hint":"Work out the charge on M first, then pair it with a chloride ion.","exp":"Two M ions balance one 2− sulfate ion, so each M carries 1+. A chloride ion is Cl⁻, so one of each cancels and the formula is MCl. The misconception is carrying the subscript across from one compound to the next and writing M₂Cl — the subscript belongs to the pair of ions, not to the metal."},{"id":"g9s-c3-v016","q":"A compound has the empirical formula CH₂. Its relative molecular mass is 56. What is its molecular formula? (C = 12, H = 1)","opts":["CH₂","C₂H₄","C₃H₆","C₄H₈"],"a":"C₄H₈","hint":"Find the mass of the empirical unit, then divide the Mr by it.","exp":"Mass of CH₂ = 12 + 2 = 14. 56 ÷ 14 = 4, so molecular formula = (CH₂)₄ = C₄H₈."},{"id":"g9s-pp24c-017","q":"What is the formula of <b>iron (III) sulfate</b>? (The sulfate ion is SO<sub>4</sub> with valency 2.)","opts":["Fe(SO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>3</sub>","Fe<sub>2</sub>(SO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>3</sub>","FeSO<sub>4</sub>","Fe<sub>3</sub>(SO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>"],"a":"Fe<sub>2</sub>(SO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>3</sub>","hint":"“Iron (III)” tells you iron’s valency is 3. Cross it with the sulfate’s valency of 2, and bracket the sulfate group.","exp":"Iron (III) has valency 3 and sulfate has valency 2, so crossing over gives two iron and three sulfate groups: <b>Fe<sub>2</sub>(SO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>3</sub></b>. The brackets are essential - without them the 3 would apply only to the oxygen."},{"id":"g9s-pp24c-027","q":"Which is the <b>balanced</b> equation for sodium reacting with water?","opts":["2Na + 2H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; 2NaOH + H<sub>2</sub>","Na + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; NaOH + H<sub>2</sub>","Na + 2H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; NaOH + 2H<sub>2</sub>","2Na + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; 2NaOH + H<sub>2</sub>"],"a":"2Na + 2H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; 2NaOH + H<sub>2</sub>","hint":"Count each kind of atom on both sides - they must match.","exp":"<b>2Na + 2H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; 2NaOH + H<sub>2</sub></b> balances: 2 Na, 4 H and 2 O on each side. The unbalanced version leaves hydrogen unequal."}]},"grade9-english":{"g9eng-listening":[{"id":"g9eng-samp-001","q":"A speaker begins: <i>\"There are three points I want to make, and the last one matters most.\"</i><br>What should a listener do with this information?","opts":["Write down only the first of the three points made","Expect three points and listen hardest for the third","Assume that the talk has already finished completely","Ignore the order in which the three points come"],"a":"Expect three points and listen hardest for the third","hint":"The speaker has just told you how the talk is organised.","exp":"A signpost like this tells you the structure in advance: three points are coming, and the speaker has flagged the third as the important one. Knowing that lets you listen and take notes with the right emphasis."},{"id":"g9eng-sb-059","q":"While listening to a talk, what is the most useful thing to <b>note down</b>?","opts":["Only the speaker's name","The length of the talk","Key words and main points","Every word the speaker says"],"a":"Key words and main points","hint":"You cannot write as fast as someone speaks.","exp":"Effective listening notes capture <b>key words and main points</b>, because writing every word means missing what comes next."},{"id":"g9eng-sb-060","q":"A speaker says: <i>\"Well, the results were... interesting.\"</i> The pause and the tone most likely suggest:","opts":["The results were expected","The results were disappointing","The results were unavailable","The results were excellent"],"a":"The results were disappointing","hint":"Why would a speaker hesitate before choosing that particular word?","exp":"The hesitation and the guarded word choice suggest the speaker is avoiding saying something negative outright, so the results were <b>disappointing</b>. Tone often carries meaning the words avoid."}],"g9eng-reading":[{"id":"g9eng-sb-002","q":"Read the extract:<br><i>\"Rajiv checked his watch again, tapped his foot, and looked down the empty road for the fourth time.\"</i><br>What can you infer about Rajiv?","opts":["He is delighted","He is exhausted","He is frightened","He is impatient"],"a":"He is impatient","hint":"The text never names the feeling. What do the three actions have in common?","exp":"Checking a watch, tapping a foot and looking down the road repeatedly all suggest <b>impatience</b>. An inference is a conclusion the reader draws from evidence, not something the writer states."},{"id":"g9eng-sb-009","q":"Read the extract:<br><i>\"Entry is free for children under twelve. Adults pay Rs 150, and students with a card pay Rs 80.\"</i><br>How much does a student with a card pay?","opts":["Nothing","Rs 120","Rs 150","Rs 80"],"a":"Rs 80","hint":"Match the category in the question to the category in the text.","exp":"The text states students with a card pay <b>Rs 80</b>. Retrieval questions are lost by reading the wrong row, not by misunderstanding the words."},{"id":"g9eng-sb-006","q":"A student writes: <i>\"The narrator is nervous.\"</i> Which quotation best <b>supports</b> that statement?","opts":["\"It was a Tuesday in April\"","\"The room was painted pale blue\"","\"My hands would not keep still\"","\"She had lived there for years\""],"a":"\"My hands would not keep still\"","hint":"Which quotation shows a physical sign of the feeling being claimed?","exp":"Hands that will not keep still are a physical sign of nerves, so that quotation is <b>evidence</b> for the claim. The others give setting or background and support nothing about the narrator's feelings."},{"id":"g9eng-sb-003","q":"Read the extract:<br><i>\"Yet another shopping centre has been approved, while the last stretch of woodland near the village quietly disappears.\"</i><br>What is the author's attitude?","opts":["Neutral about the development","Enthusiastic about the development","Critical of the development","Amused by the development"],"a":"Critical of the development","hint":"Look at \"Yet another\" and \"quietly disappears\" - what feeling do those word choices carry?","exp":"\"Yet another\" signals weariness and \"quietly disappears\" suggests something valuable is being lost unnoticed, so the author is <b>critical</b>. Identifying an author's view means reading the loaded words, not just the facts."},{"id":"g9eng-sb-007","q":"Text A calls the new bypass \"a long-overdue relief for drivers\". Text B calls it \"a scar across good farmland\". What is the main <b>difference</b> between the two texts?","opts":["They describe two different roads","They judge the same road differently","They are both written for children","They were written in different centuries"],"a":"They judge the same road differently","hint":"Both texts are about one road. Compare the attitude, not the subject.","exp":"Both describe the same bypass, but A frames it as a benefit and B as damage - the difference is one of <b>viewpoint</b>. Comparing across texts means comparing attitudes, not just content."}],"g9eng-writing":[{"id":"g9eng-d1-037","q":"Which sentence shows the reader that the market is busy, rather than telling them?","opts":["The market was extremely busy today","The busy market was full of people","It was a very busy morning there","Three voices shouted the same price"],"a":"Three voices shouted the same price","hint":"One sentence gives evidence a reader can picture; the other three only assert.","exp":"Shouted prices are something the reader can hear, so they work out &ldquo;busy&rdquo; for themselves. The other three use the word <i>busy</i> and give nothing to see or hear; putting <i>very</i> or <i>extremely</i> in front of a label does not turn it into a picture."},{"id":"g9eng-d2-054","q":"A pupil is proofreading this line of a report: <p>The inspector asked whether the fire exit had been checked</p> Which mark ends it, and why?","opts":["A question mark, because a query is reported","A full stop, because nobody is being asked","An exclamation mark, because it is a warning","A question mark, because asked is in the line"],"a":"A full stop, because nobody is being asked","hint":"Ask whether the sentence itself puts a question to the reader, or only tells you that a question was put to somebody else.","exp":"The sentence reports a question rather than asking one, so it is a statement and takes a full stop. A question mark belongs only where the words themselves ask &mdash; <i>Has the fire exit been checked?</i> &mdash; and the presence of the verb <i>asked</i> is not what decides it."},{"id":"g9eng-d1-043","q":"A description of a village square moves from a stall, to the church clock, back to that same stall, then to a dog. What is wrong with it?","opts":["The reader is moved about at random","It has been written in the past tense","The sentences are all the same length","There are too few adjectives used in it"],"a":"The reader is moved about at random","hint":"Try to draw the square in the order the details arrive.","exp":"A description needs a path the reader can follow &mdash; near to far, left to right, or the whole scene and then one corner of it. Coming back to the stall after leaving it makes the reader build the picture twice. Tense and adjective count are not the trouble; the route is."},{"id":"g9eng-d1-016","q":"A pupil plans a 60-word note with five points: the fire drill, why it was called, how long it lasted, who was late back, and a full account of every class that took part. What is wrong with this plan?","opts":["The points are in the wrong time order","The note has no greeting or sign-off","Five points is too many for any note","The last point cannot fit in 60 words"],"a":"The last point cannot fit in 60 words","hint":"Divide the words available by the number of points, then see which point refuses to shrink that far.","exp":"Sixty words across five points is about twelve words each, and a full account of every class cannot be written in twelve. The other four are each a sentence long. Five points is not too many when they are the task&rsquo;s own bullets &mdash; the fault is one point whose size does not match the space."},{"id":"g9eng-d2-044","q":"Two drafts of one sentence:<p><b>A</b> &mdash; The mayor complimented the new library.<br><b>B</b> &mdash; The mayor complemented the new library.</p> Which draft says the mayor praised it, and why?","opts":["Both drafts say exactly the same","B &mdash; a complement is praise","A &mdash; a compliment is praise","Neither spelling is a real word"],"a":"A &mdash; a compliment is praise","hint":"One of the two words is about kind things said; the other is about two things that go together.","exp":"A <i>compliment</i> is praise, so draft A is the one meant. To <i>complement</i> something is to complete or set it off, so B says the mayor somehow improved the library by being near it. Both are real words and both fit the grammar, which is why only the meaning can decide between them."}]},"grade9-french":{"g9fr-grammaire":[{"id":"g9fr-pi-015","q":"Quelle est la forme correcte de l'interrogation indirecte ? « Il demande : 'Où habites-tu ?' »","opts":["Il demande où tu habiteras.","Il demande où est-ce que tu habites.","Il demande où habites-tu.","Il demande où tu habites."],"a":"Il demande où tu habites.","hint":"Dans l'interrogation indirecte, il n'y a pas d'inversion sujet-verbe.","exp":"En interrogation indirecte, on supprime l'inversion et les marques d'interrogation directe : <i>il demande <b>où tu habites</b></i> (ordre normal sujet + verbe)."},{"id":"g9fr-pi-011","q":"Remplace « les fleurs » par le pronom correct : « Nous avons acheté les fleurs. » →","opts":["Nous y avons achetées.","Nous en avons achetées.","Nous les avons acheté.","Nous les avons achetées."],"a":"Nous les avons achetées.","hint":"COD féminin pluriel → « les ». Le participe s'accorde avec ce COD placé avant.","exp":"<i>Les fleurs</i> (COD féminin pluriel) devient <b>les</b>, placé avant le verbe. Le participe passé <b>achetées</b> s'accorde."},{"id":"g9fr-grv-085","q":"Le professeur veut les copies. Quelle forme de l’impératif est correcte ?","opts":["Me-les donnez","Donnez-moi-les","Donnez-les-moi","Les-donnez-moi"],"a":"Donnez-les-moi","hint":"À l'impératif affirmatif, COD (les) avant COI (moi).","exp":"À l'<b>impératif affirmatif</b> : COD (les) + COI (moi/toi) avec traits d'union → <b>Donnez-les-moi</b>."},{"id":"g9fr-fam-011-d","q":"Temps et modes verbaux.<br><br>Un élève écrit : « Je veux que tu fais attention. » Quelle correction convient ?","opts":["Je veux que tu fasse attention","Je veux que tu fasses attention","Je veux que tu feras attention","Je veux que tu faisais attention"],"a":"Je veux que tu fasses attention","hint":"Le verbe « vouloir que » n'introduit pas un fait, mais une volonté.","exp":"« Vouloir que » commande le <b>subjonctif</b> : « je veux que tu <b>fasses</b> attention ». Deux erreurs se croisent ici : garder l'indicatif (« feras », « faisais ») parce que la phrase paraît énoncer un fait, et oublier le -s de la 2e personne du subjonctif (« fasse »). Une volonté n'est pas un fait : le mode le montre."},{"id":"g9fr-sb-005","q":"Remplace le complément : « Je donne le livre <b>à Marie</b>. »","opts":["Je lui donne le livre","Je le donne le livre","Je leur donne le livre","Je la donne le livre"],"a":"Je lui donne le livre","hint":"« à Marie » est un complément d'objet indirect, au singulier.","exp":"Le COI singulier se remplace par <b>lui</b> : « Je <b>lui</b> donne le livre ». « Leur » serait le pluriel et « la » un COD."}],"g9fr-transformation":[{"id":"g9fr-trv-013","q":"Laquelle est correctement écrite ?","opts":["Bravo ! Tu as réussi.!","Bravo ! Tu as réussi !","Bravo !. Tu as réussi !","Bravo ! Tu as réussi ?!"],"a":"Bravo ! Tu as réussi !","hint":"On n'accumule pas deux signes de fin de phrase.","exp":"On ne cumule pas deux signes terminaux. Chaque phrase a son propre <b>point d'exclamation</b> et ils ne se doublent pas."},{"id":"g9fr-trv-048","q":"Dans « un seul homme », que signifie « seul » placé avant le nom ?","opts":["Un unique, pas d'autre","Un homme solitaire, isolé","Un homme très grand","Un homme très vieux"],"a":"Un unique, pas d'autre","hint":"« Seul » avant le nom a le sens numérique de « unique ».","exp":"<b>Seul</b> avant le nom = unique : « un <b>seul</b> homme » (pas d'autre). Après le nom = solitaire : « un homme <b>seul</b> » (sans compagnie)."},{"id":"g9fr-trv-102","q":"Transforme au passé : « Après qu'il finit son travail, il sort. »","opts":["Après qu'il a fini son travail, il sort.","Après qu'il finissait son travail, il a sorti.","Après qu'il eut fini son travail, il sortit.","Après qu'il avait fini son travail, il sortait."],"a":"Après qu'il eut fini son travail, il sortit.","hint":"Passé antérieur (action finie d'abord) + passé simple (action suivante).","exp":"Avec « après que », l'action antérieure prend le <b>passé antérieur</b> (eut fini) et la principale se met au <b>passé simple</b> (sortit)."},{"id":"g9fr-ap1-027","q":"Remplace « les affiches » par un pronom : « Il a collé les affiches devant la salle. » Quelle version est juste ?","opts":["Il les a collées devant la salle.","Il a les collées devant la salle.","Il les a collé devant la salle.","Il leur a collées devant la salle."],"a":"Il les a collées devant la salle.","hint":"Deux choses se décident : où se met le pronom par rapport à l'auxiliaire, et ce que cette place entraîne pour le participe.","exp":"Le pronom complément se place <b>devant l'auxiliaire</b>, jamais entre l'auxiliaire et le participe : « il <b>les a</b> collées ». Et comme ce COD est désormais placé avant le verbe, le participe s'accorde avec lui : « les affiches » est féminin pluriel, donc <b>collées</b>. L'erreur fréquente est de bien placer le pronom mais d'oublier l'accord qu'il déclenche."},{"id":"g9fr-ap1-035","q":"Mets cette phrase à la voix passive : « On a réparé la toiture après le cyclone. » Quelle version est juste ?","opts":["La toiture a été réparé après le cyclone.","La toiture a été réparée après le cyclone.","La toiture a été réparée par on après le cyclone.","La toiture était réparée après le cyclone."],"a":"La toiture a été réparée après le cyclone.","hint":"Ce sujet-là ne peut pas devenir complément d'agent ; vérifie ensuite que le temps n'a pas bougé et que le participe s'accorde.","exp":"Quand le sujet actif est <b>on</b>, le passif se construit <b>sans complément d'agent</b> : « par on » n'existe pas en français. Le temps est conservé — passé composé actif « a réparé » → passé composé passif <b>a été réparée</b> — et le participe s'accorde avec le nouveau sujet « la toiture », féminin singulier. L'erreur fréquente est « était réparée », un imparfait qui décrirait un état au lieu de l'action."}],"g9fr-vocabulaire":[{"id":"g9fr-vocv-064","q":"Quel adjectif qualifie un roman dont on ne peut pas se détacher ?","opts":["décevant","ennuyeux","fade","captivant"],"a":"captivant","hint":"Ce roman capture l'attention du lecteur.","exp":"<b>Captivant</b> : qui captive, retient l'attention de façon forte. Synonymes : passionnant, prenant, fascinant."},{"id":"g9fr-vocv-057","q":"Quel nom désigne le chemin étroit tracé dans une forêt ou à la campagne ?","opts":["une route","une avenue","un sentier","une allée"],"a":"un sentier","hint":"Ce chemin est étroit et souvent naturel.","exp":"Un <b>sentier</b> est un petit chemin étroit à pied dans la nature. Une route est large et goudronnée, une allée est aménagée dans un jardin ou un parc."},{"id":"g9fr-vocv-089","q":"Quel adverbe signifie « avec une extrême paresse, de façon nonchalante » ?","opts":["nonchalamment","vigoureusement","activement","énergiquement"],"a":"nonchalamment","hint":"De façon nonchalante, sans énergie ni enthousiasme.","exp":"<b>Nonchalamment</b> : avec nonchalance, mollement, sans entrain. Contraires : vigoureusement, énergiquement."},{"id":"g9fr-ap2-009","q":"Madame Sewsurn doit prouver devant un notaire qu'un terrain de Flacq lui appartient bien. Quel document doit-elle présenter ?","opts":["un devis de maçonnerie","un reçu de paiement","un bail de location","un titre de propriété"],"a":"un titre de propriété","hint":"Trois de ces documents prouvent une dépense, un projet de travaux ou un droit d'occuper — aucun ne prouve un droit de posséder.","exp":"Le <b>titre de propriété</b> est le seul document qui établit à qui appartient un bien. Un reçu prouve qu'une somme a été payée, un devis annonce le prix de travaux à venir, et un bail dit seulement qu'on a le droit d'occuper le terrain d'un autre. Payer, occuper et posséder sont trois choses différentes."},{"id":"g9fr-ap2-014","q":"La compagnie annonce que la ligne d'autobus 198 n'a pas eu une seule minute de retard de toute l'année. Quel adverbe dit exactement cela ?","opts":["éventuellement","brusquement","rarement","ponctuellement"],"a":"ponctuellement","hint":"Un seul de ces adverbes porte sur le respect de l'heure annoncée.","exp":"<b>Ponctuellement</b> veut dire « à l'heure exacte », ce que confirme « pas une seule minute de retard ». « Rarement » compte les fois, « brusquement » décrit la manière, et « éventuellement » signifie « peut-être », ce qui ne dit rien de l'heure. En français, éventuellement ne veut jamais dire « pour finir »."}]},"grade9-ict":{"g9ict-computer-systems":[{"id":"g9ict-sys-015","q":"Which list puts the units in order from <b>smallest to largest</b>?","opts":["Kilobyte, byte, bit, megabyte","Bit, byte, megabyte, kilobyte","Bit, byte, kilobyte, megabyte","Byte, bit, kilobyte, megabyte"],"a":"Bit, byte, kilobyte, megabyte","hint":"Start with the smallest piece of data there is.","exp":"A bit is the smallest unit, 8 bits make a byte, about a thousand bytes make a kilobyte and about a thousand kilobytes make a megabyte."},{"id":"g9ict-sav-112","q":"Cloud storage is a form of storage where data is:","opts":["held on remote internet servers","held on a USB drive only","held in the computer RAM","held on a Blu-ray disc at home"],"a":"held on remote internet servers","hint":"Files in the cloud, not on your device.","exp":"Cloud storage (Google Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox) saves files on provider-managed servers; users access them via the internet from any device."},{"id":"g9ict-sys-117","q":"Which of these storage capacities is the largest?","opts":["1 gigabyte","1 megabyte","1 kilobyte","1 terabyte"],"a":"1 terabyte","hint":"Work up the ladder of units.","exp":"The order is kilobyte, megabyte, gigabyte, terabyte, so a terabyte is the largest of the four."},{"id":"g9ict-sys-016","q":"Approximately how many <b>megabytes</b> are there in 3 gigabytes?","opts":["30","3000","300","30000"],"a":"3000","hint":"There are about 1000 megabytes in a gigabyte.","exp":"1 GB is about 1000 MB, so 3 GB is about 3 &times; 1000 = 3000 MB."},{"id":"g9ict-fam-010-e","q":"A club must store 6000 photographs on a single memory card and asks a technician which capacity to buy.<br><br>Which piece of information is still missing before the capacity can be worked out?","opts":["The price of the largest card the shop sells","The average size of one photograph file","The number of members who belong to the club","The make of the camera used to take them"],"a":"The average size of one photograph file","hint":"Capacity needed = number of files x size of one file. Which side is unknown?","exp":"Without the size of one file the total cannot be calculated, however many photographs there are. The other three facts are real but irrelevant to capacity, and the skill being tested is separating the information a calculation needs from the information a situation happens to supply."}],"g9ict-software-os":[{"id":"g9ict-sav-085","q":"User interface management by the OS provides:","opts":["the environment the user works in","automatic backups of user data","the connection to the internet","protection from computer viruses"],"a":"the environment the user works in","hint":"GUI or CLI — the OS provides the interface.","exp":"The OS presents either a Graphical User Interface (GUI, with icons and windows) or a Command Line Interface (CLI) through which users control the computer."},{"id":"g9ict-sav-087","q":"Security management by the OS includes:","opts":["designing the user interface","optimising the battery life","managing the network cables","login and access permissions"],"a":"login and access permissions","hint":"Login and permissions are OS security functions.","exp":"The OS authenticates users at login, enforces file and folder access permissions, and protects system resources from unauthorised programs and users."},{"id":"g9ict-sav-100","q":"Backup software is a utility that:","opts":["scans the disk for viruses","copies files to a safe place","speeds up the processor","connects the PC to the web"],"a":"copies files to a safe place","hint":"Backups are copies for disaster recovery.","exp":"Backup software automates the process of copying data to an external drive, NAS or cloud, enabling recovery after hardware failure, ransomware or accidental deletion."},{"id":"g9ict-piv-020","q":"A school wants software that can be freely used and distributed, but whose source code cannot be modified. Which type best fits?","opts":["Proprietary software","Shareware","Open source software","Freeware"],"a":"Freeware","hint":"","exp":"Freeware is software available at no charge that can be freely used and distributed, but the source code is not provided for modification. Open source software allows the source code to be viewed and changed."},{"id":"g9ict-pp24-034","q":"Which term <b>deals with the loading and running of application software</b>?","opts":["Hacking","Memory Management","Mailing list","Blog"],"a":"Memory Management","hint":"Which one is a job the operating system does?","exp":"<b>Memory management</b> is the operating system function that allocates memory to each program, loads it and reclaims the space when it closes."}],"g9ict-word-processing":[{"id":"g9ict-wpv-093","q":"Mail merge can be used to produce addressed envelopes by:","opts":["scanning a handwritten address","folding the printed letter over","choosing Envelopes as the type","typing each address on a page"],"a":"choosing Envelopes as the type","hint":"The envelope size and address position are set in the wizard.","exp":"Choosing Envelopes as the merge document type formats the page to envelope size and places address fields in the correct position."},{"id":"g9ict-piv-028","q":"In a word processing table, which key moves the cursor to the next cell?","opts":["Tab","Ctrl+Right","Enter","Spacebar"],"a":"Tab","hint":"","exp":"Pressing Tab moves the cursor to the next cell in a table. Pressing Enter within a cell creates a new line inside the same cell."},{"id":"g9ict-wpv-051","q":"Splitting a cell in a table means:","opts":["combining several cells into one","dividing it into smaller cells","removing a row from the table","adding a new column at the edge"],"a":"dividing it into smaller cells","hint":"One cell becomes several.","exp":"Split Cells divides a cell into a specified number of rows or columns."},{"id":"g9ict-wp-019","q":"Which keyboard shortcut is normally used to <b>copy</b> the selected text?","opts":["Ctrl + C","Ctrl + V","Ctrl + P","Ctrl + X"],"a":"Ctrl + C","hint":"C for copy.","exp":"Ctrl + C copies, Ctrl + X cuts and Ctrl + V pastes."},{"id":"g9ict-wp-018","q":"A newsletter must show text in two side-by-side blocks on the same page. Which feature does this?","opts":["Columns","Text boxes","Indents","Tables"],"a":"Columns","hint":"Newspapers are laid out this way.","exp":"The columns feature flows text down one block and then continues at the top of the next."}]},"grade9-maths":{"g9m-number-revision":[{"id":"g9m-my-004","q":"What is 35% of 200?","opts":["100","65","70","35"],"a":"70","hint":"Multiply 200 by 35 and divide by 100.","exp":"35% of 200 = (35 ÷ 100) × 200 = 0.35 × 200 = <b>70</b>."},{"id":"g9m-mcq-006-a","q":"Which of these is the <b>largest</b>?","opts":["0.7","0.07","0.007","0.0007"],"a":"0.7","hint":"Compare the first digit after the decimal point.","exp":"0.7 has 7 tenths; the others have 7 hundredths, thousandths and ten-thousandths. More digits after the point does not mean a bigger number."},{"id":"g9m-pp24-039","q":"A sum of money is shared between Shania and Mirella in the ratio <b>2 : 5</b>. Mirella receives <b>Rs 360 more</b> than Shania. Calculate the <b>total</b> amount shared.","opts":["Rs 1260","Rs 600","Rs 504","Rs 840"],"a":"Rs 840","hint":"The difference between the two shares is 3 parts. What is one part worth?","exp":"The shares differ by 5 &minus; 2 = 3 parts, and that difference is Rs 360, so one part is Rs 120. The total is 2 + 5 = 7 parts = 7 &times; 120 = <b>Rs 840</b>."},{"id":"g9m-pp24-024","q":"Where must brackets be inserted to make this true? <b>50 &divide; 2 &times; 5 &minus; 4 = 1</b>","opts":["50 &divide; (2 &times; 5) &minus; 4","(50 &divide; 2) &times; 5 &minus; 4","(50 &divide; 2 &times; 5) &minus; 4","50 &divide; 2 &times; (5 &minus; 4)"],"a":"50 &divide; (2 &times; 5) &minus; 4","hint":"You need the division to happen last among the first three numbers - so make 2 &times; 5 into a single divisor.","exp":"50 &divide; (2 &times; 5) &minus; 4 = 50 &divide; 10 &minus; 4 = 5 &minus; 4 = <b>1</b>. Without brackets the left-to-right rule gives 50 &divide; 2 &times; 5 &minus; 4 = 121."},{"id":"g9m-pp24-033","q":"12 men take 15 days to build a wall. How many <b>more</b> men are needed to build the same wall in <b>10 days</b>? (All men work at the same rate.)","opts":["18","3","8","6"],"a":"6","hint":"Work out the total man-days for the job first. Fewer days needs more men.","exp":"The job is 12 &times; 15 = 180 man-days. In 10 days that needs 180 &divide; 10 = 18 men. The question asks how many <b>more</b>, so 18 &minus; 12 = <b>6</b>. Answering 18 is the trap."}],"g9m-indices":[{"id":"g9m-my-011","q":"Simplify a⁶ ÷ a².","opts":["a⁴","a³","a⁸","a¹²"],"a":"a⁴","hint":"Division law: subtract the exponents.","exp":"a⁶ ÷ a² = a^(6−2) = <b>a⁴</b>."},{"id":"g9m-ind-043-a","q":"Which of the following is equal to 2<sup>&minus;3</sup>?","opts":["<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>6</sub>","<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>","&minus;6","&minus;8"],"a":"<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>","hint":"A negative index does not make the answer negative.","exp":"2<sup>&minus;3</sup> = 1 &divide; 2<sup>3</sup> = 1/8. &minus;8 comes from reading the minus as a sign on the answer; &minus;6 and 1/6 come from multiplying 2 by 3 instead of cubing it."},{"id":"g9m-ind-041-a","q":"Which of the following is equal to <i>a</i><sup>3</sup> &times; <i>a</i><sup>4</sup>?","opts":["a<sup>1</sup>","a<sup>12</sup>","2a<sup>7</sup>","a<sup>7</sup>"],"a":"a<sup>7</sup>","hint":"Multiplying powers of the same letter adds the indices.","exp":"a<sup>3</sup> &times; a<sup>4</sup> = a<sup>3+4</sup> = a<sup>7</sup>. a<sup>12</sup> comes from multiplying the indices instead of adding; a<sup>1</sup> from subtracting; 2a<sup>7</sup> from also adding the invisible coefficients."},{"id":"g9m-pp24-023","q":"Given that the square root of 5 is 2.236 and the square root of 50 is 7.071, find the <b>square root of 5000</b>.","opts":["70.71","223.6","707.1","22.36"],"a":"70.71","hint":"5000 = 100 &times; 50, and the square root of 100 is exact.","exp":"5000 = 100 &times; 50, so the square root of 5000 = 10 &times; the square root of 50 = 10 &times; 7.071 = <b>70.71</b>. Splitting off a perfect square is the whole technique here."},{"id":"g9m-mcq-008-a","q":"Which of the following equals <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>16</sub>?","opts":["2<sup>&minus;4</sup>","2<sup>&minus;2</sup>","&minus;2<sup>4</sup>","2<sup>4</sup>"],"a":"2<sup>&minus;4</sup>","hint":"A negative index means one over the positive power.","exp":"2<sup>&minus;4</sup> = 1/2<sup>4</sup> = 1/16. 2<sup>4</sup> = 16 and &minus;2<sup>4</sup> = &minus;16 both confuse a negative index with a negative answer, and 2<sup>&minus;2</sup> = 1/4 halves the index instead of using it."}],"g9m-patterns":[{"id":"g9m-my-014","q":"The nth term of a sequence is 4n − 1. What is the 5th term?","opts":["20","19","18","21"],"a":"19","hint":"Substitute n = 5 into the formula.","exp":"4(5) − 1 = 20 − 1 = <b>19</b>."},{"id":"g9m-pp24-035","q":"The matchstick pattern uses 3, 5, 7, ... matchsticks. Which figure uses <b>21 matchsticks</b>?","opts":["Figure 11","Figure 21","Figure 9","Figure 10"],"a":"Figure 10","hint":"The figure numbered n uses 2n + 1 matchsticks. Work backwards.","exp":"2n + 1 = 21 gives 2n = 20, so n = <b>10</b>. Checking: Figure 10 uses 2(10) + 1 = 21 matchsticks."},{"id":"g9m-pp24-034","q":"Matchstick figures use 3, 5, 7, ... matchsticks for Figures 1, 2, 3. How many matchsticks does <b>Figure 5</b> use?","opts":["9","11","10","13"],"a":"11","hint":"The pattern goes up by the same amount each time.","exp":"The sequence 3, 5, 7 rises by 2 each figure, so Figure 4 uses 9 and Figure 5 uses <b>11</b>."},{"id":"g9m-mcq-010-a","q":"The <i>n</i>th term of a sequence is 2<i>n</i> + 5. What is its <b>4th</b> term?","opts":["13","28","11","14"],"a":"13","hint":"Substitute n = 4.","exp":"2(4) + 5 = 13. 11 substitutes n = 3; 28 multiplies (2 + 5) by 4; 14 forgets to add the 5 and doubles 7."},{"id":"g9m-mcq-011-a","q":"In Pascal&rsquo;s triangle, which row comes after 1, 2, 1?","opts":["2, 3, 3, 2","1, 2, 2, 1","1, 3, 2, 1","1, 3, 3, 1"],"a":"1, 3, 3, 1","hint":"Each entry is the sum of the two above it.","exp":"Adding neighbouring pairs of 1, 2, 1 gives 3 and 3, with a 1 at each end: 1, 3, 3, 1. Every row of Pascal&rsquo;s triangle begins and ends with 1."}]},"grade9-physics":{"g9s-p1-measurements":[{"id":"g9s-p1-v037","q":"A student reads the volume on a measuring cylinder from above the liquid level. Will the reading be too high or too low?","opts":["Too low","Correct","It depends on the liquid","Too high"],"a":"Too high","hint":"Looking down from above shifts the apparent level upward.","exp":"Looking from above makes the line of sight cut the scale higher than the actual meniscus level, giving a reading that is too high."},{"id":"g9s-p1-v004","q":"A bag of rice has a mass of 2 500 g. What is this mass in kilograms?","opts":["0.25 kg","25 kg","250 kg","2.5 kg"],"a":"2.5 kg","hint":"Divide by 1 000 to convert grams to kilograms.","exp":"2 500 &divide; 1 000 = 2.5 kg."},{"id":"g9s-p1-v020","q":"Where on the meniscus should a student read the volume in a measuring cylinder?","opts":["Halfway up the curve","At the bottom of the curved surface","At the top of the curved surface","At the midpoint of the liquid from outside"],"a":"At the bottom of the curved surface","hint":"Water curves downward in glass.","exp":"The correct reading is at the lowest point of the meniscus, which is the bottom of the curve."},{"id":"g9s-p1-v012","q":"A wire has a diameter of 0.05 cm. What is this diameter in millimetres?","opts":["50 mm","5 mm","0.05 mm","0.5 mm"],"a":"0.5 mm","hint":"Multiply by 10 to convert cm to mm.","exp":"0.05 &times; 10 = 0.5 mm."},{"id":"g9s-p1-v009","q":"A distance is measured as 3.6 km. What is this distance in metres?","opts":["36 m","36 000 m","3 600 m","360 m"],"a":"3 600 m","hint":"Multiply by 1 000 to convert km to m.","exp":"3.6 &times; 1 000 = 3 600 m."}],"g9s-p2-light":[{"id":"g9s-p2-006","q":"Which of these gives out its own light?","opts":["A star","A cloud","A planet","A moon"],"a":"A star","hint":"Only one of these is luminous.","exp":"A star produces its own light; planets, moons and clouds are seen by reflected light."},{"id":"g9s-p2-016","q":"From which line is the angle of incidence measured?","opts":["The mirror surface","The normal","The edge of the mirror","The reflected ray"],"a":"The normal","hint":"Not from the mirror itself.","exp":"Both the angle of incidence and the angle of reflection are measured from the normal, not from the mirror surface."},{"id":"g9s-pdm-007","q":"A man stands <b>48 cm</b> in front of a plane mirror. What is the total distance between the <b>man</b> and his <b>image</b>?","opts":["24 cm","192 cm","96 cm","48 cm"],"a":"96 cm","hint":"The image is as far behind the mirror as the object is in front.","exp":"In a plane mirror, the image distance equals the object distance. The man is 48 cm in front of the mirror, so his image is 48 cm <b>behind</b> the mirror. The total distance between the man and his image = 48 + 48 = <b>96 cm</b>."},{"id":"g9s-p2-v074","q":"A ray of light hits a plane mirror. The angle between the incident ray and the mirror surface is 30&deg;. Using the law of reflection, what is the angle between the reflected ray and the mirror surface?","opts":["90&deg;","45&deg;","30&deg;","60&deg;"],"a":"30&deg;","hint":"Angle to surface = 90 &minus; angle to normal. If angle of incidence = 60&deg; then angle of reflection = 60&deg;, giving 30&deg; to the surface.","exp":"Angle of incidence = 90 &minus; 30 = 60&deg;. Angle of reflection = 60&deg;. Angle between reflected ray and surface = 90 &minus; 60 = 30&deg;."},{"id":"g9s-p2-018","q":"The second law of reflection states that the incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal:","opts":["All lie in the same plane","Are always parallel to each other","Are always at right angles","Always meet the mirror at 45&deg;"],"a":"All lie in the same plane","hint":"It is about them lying flat together, not about their sizes.","exp":"The second law states that the incident ray, the normal and the reflected ray all lie in the same plane."}],"g9s-p3-energy":[{"id":"g9s-p3-v009","q":"In real situations, a falling ball does not convert all its GPE into KE. Where does the missing energy go?","opts":["It disappears because energy is not always conserved","Into the gravitational field of the Earth","Into heat and sound due to air resistance","Back into GPE at the bottom"],"a":"Into heat and sound due to air resistance","hint":"Energy is always conserved; the &ldquo;missing&rdquo; energy must be somewhere.","exp":"Air resistance converts some energy into heat and sound; the total energy (KE + GPE + heat + sound) is still conserved."},{"id":"g9s-p3-v029","q":"A 2 kg book is placed on a shelf 1.5 m above the floor. Taking g = 10 N/kg, what is its GPE relative to the floor?","opts":["30 J","15 J","300 J","3 J"],"a":"30 J","hint":"GPE = mgh.","exp":"GPE = 2 &times; 10 &times; 1.5 = 30 J."},{"id":"g9s-p3-v007","q":"A pendulum bob of mass 0.2 kg is raised 0.45 m above its lowest point. Taking g = 10 N/kg, what is the maximum speed it reaches at the lowest point (ignoring air resistance)?","opts":["9 m/s","4.5 m/s","0.9 m/s","3 m/s"],"a":"3 m/s","hint":"Set mgh = &frac12;mv&sup2; and solve for v.","exp":"mgh = &frac12;mv&sup2;; v = &radic;(2gh) = &radic;(2 &times; 10 &times; 0.45) = &radic;9 = 3 m/s."},{"id":"g9s-p3-v023","q":"A 4 kg object is dropped from a height and reaches the ground at 6 m/s. Taking g = 10 N/kg, from what height was it dropped (ignoring air resistance)?","opts":["3.6 m","1.8 m","2.4 m","0.9 m"],"a":"1.8 m","hint":"Set mgh = &frac12;mv&sup2; and solve for h.","exp":"h = v&sup2; &divide; (2g) = 36 &divide; 20 = 1.8 m."},{"id":"g9s-p3te-019","q":"An engineer designs a 60 m steel footbridge for a site where the temperature runs from 12 degrees at night to 38 degrees at midday. Which design decision follows from thermal expansion?","opts":["Bolt both ends firmly to their concrete supports","Build it as one piece with no joints anywhere","Paint the steel dark so it takes in more heat","Fix one end and let the other slide on rollers"],"a":"Fix one end and let the other slide on rollers","hint":"The deck has to be free to change length somewhere.","exp":"Steel lengthens as the day warms. Fixing one end and mounting the other on rollers lets the deck grow and shrink freely; bolting both ends builds up forces large enough to buckle the deck or crack a support."}]},"grade9-social-modern-studies":{"g9sms-colonial-independence":[{"id":"g9sms-dpa-026","q":"Where was the constitutional conference held in 1965 at which the terms of Mauritian independence were discussed?","opts":["In Port Louis","In London","In New Delhi","In Geneva"],"a":"In London","hint":"It was held in the capital of the colonial power of the day.","exp":"The 1965 constitutional conference was held in <b>London</b>, since Britain was the power that had to agree to the transfer. Port Louis is the tempting answer because the outcome mattered most here, but the conference itself was convened by the British government."},{"id":"g9sms-my002","q":"Who was the leader of the <i>Parti Mauricien Social Démocrate</i> (PMSD) during the 1967 elections?","opts":["Sir Anerood Jugnauth","Sir Abdool Raman Osman","Sir Seewoosagur Ramgoolam","Sir Gaëtan Duval"],"a":"Sir Gaëtan Duval","hint":"He was known for his charismatic opposition to independence.","exp":"<b>Sir Gaëtan Duval</b> led the PMSD, which campaigned against independence. The ILP under Sir Seewoosagur Ramgoolam won the 1967 election, securing independence."},{"id":"g9sms-pi018","q":"In which year was slavery abolished in Mauritius?","opts":["1810","1868","1835","1815"],"a":"1835","hint":"This was two years after the British Slavery Abolition Act was passed.","exp":"Slavery was abolished in Mauritius in <b>1835</b> under British colonial rule, following the Slavery Abolition Act of 1833. Slaveholders received compensation; the enslaved people received none and were bound to an \"apprenticeship\" system for several more years."},{"id":"g9sms-sb-002","q":"In the 1967 general election, what was the main question put to voters?","opts":["Whether to build a new airport","Whether to join the Commonwealth","Whether to change the national language","Whether Mauritius should become independent"],"a":"Whether Mauritius should become independent","hint":"The election was effectively a vote on the island's future status.","exp":"The 1967 election was fought between the Independence Party, which won, and the PMSD, which favoured association with Britain. It settled the question of <b>independence</b>."},{"id":"g9sms-pi025","q":"Which treaty, signed in 1814, formally confirmed British sovereignty over Mauritius?","opts":["The Treaty of Amiens","The Treaty of Vienna","The Treaty of Versailles","The Treaty of Paris"],"a":"The Treaty of Paris","hint":"This is not the 1783 treaty — the 1814 version ended the Napoleonic Wars.","exp":"The <b>Treaty of Paris (1814)</b> confirmed British control over Mauritius, which Britain had captured from France in 1810. Britain agreed to respect French settlers' language, religion and civil law — the basis of today's dual French–English heritage."}],"g9sms-living-conditions":[{"id":"g9sms-my006","q":"Which practical benefit for students followed the student movement of 1975 in Mauritius?","opts":["Free bus transport for students was introduced","A new national university was founded","The price of sugar exports was fixed by law","Secondary school fees were doubled nationwide"],"a":"Free bus transport for students was introduced","hint":"It was a practical benefit that students demanded.","exp":"After the <b>1975 student movement</b> the government introduced <b>free bus transport</b> for students, which is still in place. The bigger demand behind the march — free secondary education — followed in 1977."},{"id":"g9sms-sb-005","q":"Which disease was a major killer in Mauritius before the 1950s eradication campaign?","opts":["Malaria","Cholera only","Influenza only","Measles only"],"a":"Malaria","hint":"It is spread by mosquitoes breeding in standing water.","exp":"<b>Malaria</b> killed thousands each year until a campaign of spraying and drainage eradicated it in the 1950s. Its removal is one reason life expectancy rose so sharply."},{"id":"g9sms-pi060","q":"What type of houses did most ordinary Mauritian families live in during the 1940s?","opts":["Concrete block houses","Stone-walled houses","Straw (thatched) houses","Wooden houses with tiled roofs"],"a":"Straw (thatched) houses","hint":"Housing was basic; the economy was poor at that time.","exp":"In the 1940s, most ordinary Mauritian families lived in simple <b>straw or thatched houses</b>. These were cheap to build but offered little protection from cyclones or heavy rain. Concrete housing became more widespread from the 1960s and 1970s as living standards improved."},{"id":"g9sms-sb-007","q":"Before free secondary education, what mainly decided whether a child continued to secondary school?","opts":["The child's age in years","The size of the school building","The child's district of birth","Whether the family could pay the fees"],"a":"Whether the family could pay the fees","hint":"Secondary schooling was not free at the time.","exp":"Secondary education was <b>fee-paying</b>, so ability to pay decided who continued. Free secondary education from 1977 opened it to all."},{"id":"g9sms-sb-008","q":"What were students demanding in the movement of <b>20 May 1975</b>?","opts":["A change of national flag","A new international airport","Lower prices for imported cars","Free and accessible secondary education"],"a":"Free and accessible secondary education","hint":"The demand was about who could get into secondary school.","exp":"The student movement of <b>20 May 1975</b> demanded access to secondary education. Free secondary education followed in 1977."}],"g9sms-economy-1960s":[{"id":"g9sms-samp-001","q":"What was the main weakness of the Mauritian economy in the 1960s?","opts":["It relied on heavy industry","It depended on a single crop","It had no working population","It had no access to the sea"],"a":"It depended on a single crop","hint":"Think about what almost all export earnings came from.","exp":"Almost all export earnings came from sugar, so a bad harvest or a fall in the world sugar price hit the whole economy at once. Diversifying away from that dependence was the central economic task after independence."},{"id":"g9sms-sb-009","q":"At independence, the Mauritian economy depended almost entirely on which crop?","opts":["Sugar cane","Coffee grown for export","Tea grown on the plateau","Cotton woven for export"],"a":"Sugar cane","hint":"It covered most of the cultivated land on the island.","exp":"<b>Sugar cane</b> dominated exports and employment - a monocrop economy, which is exactly what made the country vulnerable to one bad price or one cyclone."},{"id":"g9sms-my010","q":"Which economic strategy did Mauritius first adopt after independence to reduce reliance on sugar?","opts":["Nationalisation of all industries","Export of raw materials only","Agricultural diversification only","Import Substitution Industrialisation (ISI)"],"a":"Import Substitution Industrialisation (ISI)","hint":"The idea was to make locally what had previously been imported.","exp":"Mauritius adopted <b>Import Substitution Industrialisation (ISI)</b> — producing goods locally that were previously imported — as its first strategy. When this proved insufficient, it pivoted to export-led growth through the EPZ."},{"id":"g9sms-sb-010","q":"Which was a major <b>obstacle</b> to development in Mauritius in the 1960s?","opts":["A complete lack of schools","Rapid population growth with few jobs","A shortage of arable land for sugar","The absence of any port facilities"],"a":"Rapid population growth with few jobs","hint":"Think about the number of young people entering the workforce.","exp":"The population had grown quickly after malaria was eradicated, so many young people entered a job market that only sugar supplied - producing high <b>unemployment</b>."},{"id":"g9sms-sb-012","q":"Which early measure helped Mauritius respond to the problems predicted in the 1960s?","opts":["A family planning programme","The closing of the sugar estates","An end to primary schooling","A ban on all imports"],"a":"A family planning programme","hint":"The prediction was about population; the response addressed it directly.","exp":"A <b>family planning</b> programme slowed population growth sharply, which together with diversifying the economy defused the crisis Meade had forecast."}]}}};
